poem:
I WANT TO DIE
If envious hate roots out the people,
Songs for the years;
Still thoughts are stirred to wakefulness;
because they are sung.
Not as soldiers, but as men.

Cous' Sun sits in silent pride.
And some called it the resurrection flower;
And sinks into my heart.
Do not cheer: once joyous maidens,
Men grasped the writing;
"I hear them call," cried the dying man,

years of strife,
And asked to enter in;
But you have gentle compassion;
Your hate of vice a sham,
When she saw her birth;

Where war has sharpened his weapons,
Stood frowning there, a cave here, bred
six
Who made the gallows an altar high,
Thy signals, ever and anon she cried;
But would please the weak frail flesh;

And careworn brows forget.
Let me dream
Of captive wind that moans for fields and
seas;
Sickle comes and leaves us stunned and dazed;
